    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(11))  # C0..C10\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(84, 2), (16, 14), (6, 28), (34, 99), (28, 29)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(27, 64), (76, 78), (95, 33), (67, 84), (64, 57), (41, 33), (43, 1), (61, 10), (36, 18), (51, 65), (97, 75)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [100.0, 76.0, 193.0, 133.0, 191.0]\n    customer_demands = [40.0, 17.0, 32.0, 15.0, 13.0, 47.0, 34.0, 33.0, 33.0, 18.0, 50.0]\n    fixed_costs = [178.0, 421.0, 154.0, 176.0, 474.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse openings and shipping plan to minimize total logistics cost\n\nWe are evaluating our distribution network to decide which of the five candidate warehouses to open and how to route product to our eleven customer locations at the lowest possible total cost. The candidate facility locations are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (84, 2)\n  - F1: (16, 14)\n  - F2: (6, 28)\n  - F3: (34, 99)\n  - F4: (28, 29), and the customer locations are detailed in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (27, 64)\n  - C1: (76, 78)\n  - C2: (95, 33)\n  - C3: (67, 84)\n  - C4: (64, 57)\n  - C5: (41, 33)\n  - C6: (43, 1)\n  - C7: (61, 10)\n  - C8: (36, 18)\n  - C9: (51, 65)\n  - C10: (97, 75). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates; all shipping cost estimates will use that distance metric.\n\nWhat we know\n- Opening a warehouse creates a fixed cost commitment; these amounts are summarized in [178.0, 421.0, 154.0, 176.0, 474.0]. A site must be opened before it can ship product.\n- Each warehouse has a finite throughput/handling limit, given in [100.0, 76.0, 193.0, 133.0, 191.0].\n- Each customer has a required volume that must be fully satisfied, as specified in [40.0, 17.0, 32.0, 15.0, 13.0, 47.0, 34.0, 33.0, 33.0, 18.0, 50.0].\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance and volume. The per-unit, per-kilometer transport rate is 0.5. This rate is applied to the straight-line distance between an open warehouse and a customer for the units shipped on that lane.\n\nBusiness objective\n- Choose which warehouses to open and define how much each open warehouse ships to each customer so that:\n  - Every customer\u2019s demand is met in full.\n  - No warehouse exceeds its capacity.\n  - We spend as little as possible in total, combining fixed opening costs and variable shipping spend.\n\nHow we will approach the decision\n- We will balance the trade-off between opening more sites (which raises fixed costs but can shorten haul distances) and consolidating into fewer sites (which lowers fixed costs but can increase shipping distances and risk capacity bottlenecks).\n- We will use the Euclidean distances derived from the map coordinates to estimate transport spend and identify which customer\u2013warehouse pairings are most cost-effective.\n- The result will be a recommended set of warehouses to open, a shipment allocation plan from those sites to each customer, and an estimate of the total cost under that plan.\n\nNext steps\n- Validate the completeness and accuracy of Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (84, 2)\n  - F1: (16, 14)\n  - F2: (6, 28)\n  - F3: (34, 99)\n  - F4: (28, 29), Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (27, 64)\n  - C1: (76, 78)\n  - C2: (95, 33)\n  - C3: (67, 84)\n  - C4: (64, 57)\n  - C5: (41, 33)\n  - C6: (43, 1)\n  - C7: (61, 10)\n  - C8: (36, 18)\n  - C9: (51, 65)\n  - C10: (97, 75), [100.0, 76.0, 193.0, 133.0, 191.0], [40.0, 17.0, 32.0, 15.0, 13.0, 47.0, 34.0, 33.0, 33.0, 18.0, 50.0], [178.0, 421.0, 154.0, 176.0, 474.0], and 0.5.\n- Run the network evaluation and present recommendations, including sensitivity to changes in demand, capacity, or transport rate.",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse openings and shipping plan to minimize total logistics cost\n\nWe are evaluating our distribution network to decide which of the five candidate warehouses to open and how to route product to our eleven customer locations at the lowest possible total cost. The candidate facility locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer locations are detailed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates; all shipping cost estimates will use that distance metric.\n\nWhat we know\n- Opening a warehouse creates a fixed cost commitment; these amounts are summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}. A site must be opened before it can ship product.\n- Each warehouse has a finite throughput/handling limit, given in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Each customer has a required volume that must be fully satisfied, as specified in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance and volume. The per-unit, per-kilometer transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}. This rate is applied to the straight-line distance between an open warehouse and a customer for the units shipped on that lane.\n\nBusiness objective\n- Choose which warehouses to open and define how much each open warehouse ships to each customer so that:\n  - Every customer\u2019s demand is met in full.\n  - No warehouse exceeds its capacity.\n  - We spend as little as possible in total, combining fixed opening costs and variable shipping spend.\n\nHow we will approach the decision\n- We will balance the trade-off between opening more sites (which raises fixed costs but can shorten haul distances) and consolidating into fewer sites (which lowers fixed costs but can increase shipping distances and risk capacity bottlenecks).\n- We will use the Euclidean distances derived from the map coordinates to estimate transport spend and identify which customer\u2013warehouse pairings are most cost-effective.\n- The result will be a recommended set of warehouses to open, a shipment allocation plan from those sites to each customer, and an estimate of the total cost under that plan.\n\nNext steps\n- Validate the completeness and accuracy of {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}, {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}, {FIXED_COSTS}, and {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n- Run the network evaluation and present recommendations, including sensitivity to changes in demand, capacity, or transport rate."
